# G013 — Credit Card Validator

*v2. See `credit-card.md` for prose: Luhn as cheap structural `<-`
gate, network identification as prefix-dispatch, structural validity
distinct from semantic validity.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Payment Intake

[payment-intake @transaction
    IN ORDER TO
        reject malformed card numbers via Luhn before any network call
        dispatch the valid card to its network-specific processor
        and verify the charge confirmation came back genuine

    <- [validate-card {number: @transaction:card-number}]
        where valid is true
        otherwise -> #card-rejected

    @network <- [identify-network {number: @transaction:card-number}]

    @charge <- @network
        where is "Visa"       -> (@VisaProcessor){charge @transaction:amount}
        where is "Mastercard" -> (@MCProcessor)  {charge @transaction:amount}
        where is "Amex"       -> (@AmexProcessor){charge @transaction:amount,
                                                    verify premium status}
        where is "Discover"   -> (@DiscoverProcessor){charge @transaction:amount}
        otherwise              -> #unsupported-network

    <- @eliana{verify charge confirmation is genuine}
]

---

## Verb Surface

[luhn-check
    {number}                                ;; -> Bool
]

[identify-network
    {number}                                 ;; -> "Visa"|"Mastercard"|...
]

[validate-card
    {number}                                 ;; -> {valid, network, ...}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: `<-` gate filters cheap `where` Luhn fails

[<- [validate-card {number: @n}] where valid is true]
    IN ORDER TO
        reject structurally-invalid card numbers before any remote call
        because Luhn is microseconds and a processor call is milliseconds

    ascending cost: gates are ordered cheap → expensive
    regex (ns) → luhn (μs) → network-dispatch (μs) → remote-processor (ms)
]

Structural validity ≠ semantic validity. Luhn catches typos and
transpositions; it does NOT prove the card exists. That's the
processor's job — and the `<-` gate protects the processor from
wasted calls on invalid input.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: network dispatch as prefix `where`

[identify-network {number: @n}]
    @n's first digits
        where begin with 4               -> "Visa"
        where begin with 51-55 or 2221-2720 -> "Mastercard"
        where begin with 34 or 37         -> "Amex"
        where begin with 6011 or 65       -> "Discover"
        otherwise                          -> "Unknown"
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [luhn-check {number: "4532015112830366"}]      ;; -> true
    -> [luhn-check {number: "4532015112830367"}]      ;; -> false

    -> [identify-network {number: "4532015112830366"}]  ;; -> "Visa"
    -> [identify-network {number: "5425233430109903"}]  ;; -> "Mastercard"
]
